2. SETUP
================================================================================

2.1 The Hodge complex
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The substrate is the periodic square lattice Lambda = Z_8 x Z_8 with
N = 64 sites (HCSM-01 [HCSM-01]). The Hodge complex of differential
forms is

    Omega^0 = R^64, Omega^1 = R^128, Omega^2 = R^64,                  (8)

of total dimension 256 (HCSM-02 [HCSM-02]). The Hodge-Dirac operator
D = d + d* satisfies D^2|_{Omega^k} = Delta exactly on each form degree.

At the self-paired eigenvalue lambda = 4, the Hodge complex decomposes as
(HCSM-02 [HCSM-02], HCSM-05 [HCSM-05]):

    Omega^0|_{lambda=4} = 3A_1 + A_2 + 2B_1 + 2B_2 + 3E  (14),       (9)

    Omega^1|_{lambda=4} = 3A_1 + 3A_2 + 3B_1 + 3B_2 + 8E  (28),     (10)

    Omega^2|_{lambda=4} = 3A_1 + A_2 + 2B_1 + 2B_2 + 3E  (14).      (11)

The full 56-dimensional sector at lambda = 4 is

    V_56 = Omega^0|_{lambda=4} + Omega^1|_{lambda=4}
           + Omega^2|_{lambda=4},                                    (12)

with D4 decomposition

    V_56 = 9A_1 + 5A_2 + 7B_1 + 7B_2 + 14E.                          (13)

Note that Omega^0|_{lambda=4} and Omega^2|_{lambda=4} have identical
D4 decompositions. They are Hodge-dual to each other.

2.2 The Hodge star
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The discrete Hodge star on the 2-torus Lambda acts on form degrees as

    star_0 : Omega^0 -> Omega^2,
    star_1 : Omega^1 -> Omega^1,
    star_2 : Omega^2 -> Omega^0.                                     (14)

With the standard convention on Omega^1 (rotating the two vector
components), the involutions are

    star_0 star_2 = id_{Omega^0},
    star_2 star_0 = id_{Omega^2},
    star_1^2 = -id_{Omega^1}.                                        (15)

The Hodge star commutes with D^2 and preserves the eigenvalue eigenspaces
(HCSM-02, HCSM-03); hence it preserves V_56.

The Hodge star provides a canonical identification Omega^0 = Omega^2:
the constant 0-form is identified with the constant 2-form, and in
general a form alpha in Omega^0 is identified with star_0 alpha in
Omega^2. This identification is D4-equivariant (HCSM-02).

2.3 The D4 action
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The D4 point group of the square acts on the Hodge complex preserving
each form degree (HCSM-02). We denote the action by R = rho(r) and
S = rho(s) on V_56.

Remark. The D4 actions on Omega^0 and Omega^2 are both site-permutation
actions induced by the point group of the square. Because the Hodge
star identification Omega^0 = Omega^2 is D4-equivariant, the D4 action
on Omega^2 coincides with the D4 action on Omega^0 under this
identification.

2.4 Sector operators
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

On V_56, define the three sector operators (HCSM-06 [HCSM-06]):

    I = P_{Omega^0} + P_{Omega^1} + P_{Omega^2},
    F = P_{Omega^0} - P_{Omega^1} + P_{Omega^2},
    O = P_{Omega^0} - P_{Omega^2}.                                   (16)

Restricted to the three sectors, their signatures are

    I|_sectors = (1, 1, 1),
    F|_sectors = (1, -1, 1),
    O|_sectors = (1, 0, -1).                                         (17)

2.5 The hypercharge functional
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The hypercharge functional is a specific element of the 15-dimensional
sector-coarse space (HCSM-07 [HCSM-07]):

    Y = sum_rho (alpha_rho I + beta_rho F + gamma_rho O) P_rho,      (18)

with A_1 and B_1 coefficients uniquely determined by the Standard Model
content and the sector assignment:

    (alpha_{A_1}, beta_{A_1}, gamma_{A_1}) = (0, -1/6, -1/2),        (19)

    (alpha_{B_1}, beta_{B_1}, gamma_{B_1}) = (0, +1/2, +1/2).        (20)

The corresponding sector hypercharge values are

    Y_{A_1}: (-2/3, +1/6, +1/3),
    Y_{B_1}: (+1, -1/2, 0).                                          (21)


================================================================================
3. THE SECTOR FLIP sigma
================================================================================

Definition 3.1 (Sector flip). The sector flip sigma : V_56 -> V_56 is
the R-linear map

    sigma(alpha_0 + omega_1 + alpha_2)
        = star_0 alpha_2 + omega_1 + star_2 alpha_0,                  (22)

where alpha_0 in Omega^0|_{lambda=4}, omega_1 in Omega^1|_{lambda=4},
alpha_2 in Omega^2|_{lambda=4}, and star_0, star_2 are the Hodge star
maps defined in (14).

Remark (Simplification). Because star_0 and star_2 are the identity maps
on their respective sectors (HCSM-02), the flip takes the simple form

    sigma(alpha_0, omega_1, alpha_2) = (alpha_2, omega_1, alpha_0).   (23)

That is, sigma swaps the Omega^0 and Omega^2 sectors while fixing
Omega^1 pointwise.

Remark (Topological character). The flip sigma is defined using the
Hodge star, which depends only on the orientation of the torus, not on
a metric. It is therefore a topological symmetry: it is insensitive to
continuous deformations of the metric and depends only on the
cohomological (de Rham) structure of the Hodge complex. This is the
"gauge topology" content of the paper's title.


================================================================================
4. INVOLUTION
================================================================================

Lemma 4.1 (Involution). sigma^2 = I_56.

Proof. For v = (alpha_0, omega_1, alpha_2) in V_56,

    sigma^2(v) = sigma(alpha_2, omega_1, alpha_0)
               = (alpha_0, omega_1, alpha_2) = v.

Hence sigma is an involution. [Box]

Corollary 4.2 (Z2 action). sigma generates a Z2 action on V_56: the
group Z2 = {e, sigma} acts on V_56 by e . v = v and sigma . v = sigma(v).

Remark (Non-degeneracy). sigma != I_56, since sigma exchanges nontrivial
subspaces Omega^0|_{lambda=4} != Omega^2|_{lambda=4} (as subspaces of
V_56). The Z2 action is therefore faithful.


================================================================================
5. D4-EQUIVARIANCE
================================================================================

Theorem 5.1 (D4-equivariance). The sector flip commutes with the D4
action on V_56:

    [sigma, R] = [sigma, S] = 0.                                     (24)

Proof. It suffices to check the two generators R = rho(r) and S = rho(s)
on each sector.

On Omega^0 + Omega^2: The D4 actions on Omega^0 and Omega^2 are
site-permutation actions induced by the point group of the square.
Because the Hodge star identification Omega^0 = Omega^2 is
D4-equivariant (HCSM-02, Proposition 2.5), the two actions coincide
under this identification. Hence for v = (alpha_0, 0, alpha_2),

    sigma R v = sigma(R alpha_0, 0, R alpha_2) = (R alpha_2, 0, R alpha_0)
              = R (alpha_2, 0, alpha_0) = R sigma v.

The same computation holds for S.

On Omega^1: sigma acts trivially on Omega^1, so

    sigma R v = sigma(R omega_1) = R omega_1 = R sigma v

for v = (0, omega_1, 0). The same for S.

Combining the two cases, sigma commutes with R and S on V_56. [Box]

Corollary 5.2 (Sector-coarse compatibility). The flip sigma preserves
the D4 isotypic structure of V_56. In particular, sigma commutes with
the isotypic projectors P_rho for all rho in {A_1, A_2, B_1, B_2, E}.


================================================================================
6. SECTOR-OPERATOR TRANSFORMATION
================================================================================

Theorem 6.1 (Sector-operator transformation). Under the flip sigma, the
sector operators transform as

    sigma I sigma^{-1} = I,
    sigma F sigma^{-1} = F,
    sigma O sigma^{-1} = -O.                                         (25)

Proof. The sector projections transform under sigma as

    sigma P_{Omega^0} sigma^{-1} = P_{Omega^2},
    sigma P_{Omega^1} sigma^{-1} = P_{Omega^1},
    sigma P_{Omega^2} sigma^{-1} = P_{Omega^0}.                      (26)

(Conjugating P_{Omega^0} by sigma gives the projector onto the image
sigma(Omega^0) = Omega^2, and similarly for the other sectors.)

Then:

    sigma I sigma^{-1}
        = sigma P_{Omega^0} sigma^{-1}
        + sigma P_{Omega^1} sigma^{-1}
        + sigma P_{Omega^2} sigma^{-1}
        = P_{Omega^2} + P_{Omega^1} + P_{Omega^0} = I.

    sigma F sigma^{-1}
        = sigma P_{Omega^0} sigma^{-1}
        - sigma P_{Omega^1} sigma^{-1}
        + sigma P_{Omega^2} sigma^{-1}
        = P_{Omega^2} - P_{Omega^1} + P_{Omega^0} = F.

    sigma O sigma^{-1}
        = sigma P_{Omega^0} sigma^{-1}
        - sigma P_{Omega^2} sigma^{-1}
        = P_{Omega^2} - P_{Omega^0} = -O.

[Box]

Corollary 6.2 (Algebraic transformation). The flip sigma acts on the
sector-coarse algebra as the involution

    (alpha, beta, gamma) -> (alpha, beta, -gamma)                    (27)

on the coefficients of alpha I + beta F + gamma O.

Proof. Immediate from Theorem 6.1: the span of {I, F, O} is preserved
by sigma, with the stated action on coordinates. [Box]


