7. HYPERCHARGE FUNCTIONAL TRANSFORMATION
================================================================================

7.1 A_1 coefficient transformation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Theorem 7.1 (A_1 coefficient transformation). Under sigma, the A_1
hypercharge coefficients transform as

    (alpha_{A_1}, beta_{A_1}, gamma_{A_1})
        = (0, -1/6, -1/2) -> (0, -1/6, +1/2).                        (28)

Proof. The flip acts on the sector-coarse algebra as
(alpha, beta, gamma) -> (alpha, beta, -gamma). Applying this to the A_1
coefficients from (19):

    (0, -1/6, -1/2) -> (0, -1/6, +1/2).

The projectors P_rho are invariant under sigma (Theorem 5.1), so the
action on the full functional is well-defined. [Box]

7.2 B_1 coefficient transformation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Theorem 7.2 (B_1 coefficient transformation). Under sigma, the B_1
hypercharge coefficients transform as

    (alpha_{B_1}, beta_{B_1}, gamma_{B_1})
        = (0, +1/2, +1/2) -> (0, +1/2, -1/2).                        (29)

Proof. Same as Theorem 7.1 applied to the B_1 coefficients from (20).
[Box]

7.3 Sector-value swap
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Theorem 7.3 (Sector-value swap). Under sigma, the sector hypercharge
values transform by swapping Omega^0 and Omega^2:

    Y_{A_1}: (-2/3, +1/6, +1/3) -> (+1/3, +1/6, -2/3),               (30)

    Y_{B_1}: (+1, -1/2, 0) -> (0, -1/2, +1).                         (31)

Proof. Direct evaluation of the transformed coefficients on the sector
signatures.

For A_1 with transformed coefficients (0, -1/6, +1/2):

    Y_{A_1}'(Omega^0) = 0 + (-1/6) + (+1/2) = +1/3,

    Y_{A_1}'(Omega^1) = 0 - (-1/6) = +1/6,

    Y_{A_1}'(Omega^2) = 0 + (-1/6) - (+1/2) = -2/3.

So Y_{A_1} on (Omega^0, Omega^1, Omega^2) transforms from
(-2/3, +1/6, +1/3) to (+1/3, +1/6, -2/3).

For B_1 with transformed coefficients (0, +1/2, -1/2):

    Y_{B_1}'(Omega^0) = 0 + (+1/2) + (-1/2) = 0,

    Y_{B_1}'(Omega^1) = 0 - (+1/2) = -1/2,

    Y_{B_1}'(Omega^2) = 0 + (+1/2) - (-1/2) = +1.

So Y_{B_1} transforms from (+1, -1/2, 0) to (0, -1/2, +1).

In both cases, the transformation is exactly the swap
Omega^0 <-> Omega^2. [Box]


