8.1 Magnitude-multiset invariance
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Corollary 8.1 (Magnitude multiset invariant). The multiset of
hypercharge magnitudes

    {|Y_{A_1}(Omega^0)|, |Y_{A_1}(Omega^1)|, |Y_{A_1}(Omega^2)|}
        = {2/3, 1/6, 1/3}                                            (32)

on the A_1 block and

    {|Y_{B_1}(Omega^0)|, |Y_{B_1}(Omega^1)|, |Y_{B_1}(Omega^2)|}
        = {1, 1/2, 0}                                                (33)

on the B_1 block are invariant under sigma.

Proof. By Theorem 7.3, the flip swaps Omega^0 and Omega^2 while fixing
Omega^1. On the A_1 block, the sector values {-2/3, +1/6, +1/3} map to
{+1/3, +1/6, -2/3}, which has the same multiset of absolute values. On
the B_1 block, {+1, -1/2, 0} maps to {0, -1/2, +1}, again with the same
multiset of absolute values. [Box]

8.2 Relabeling of color-triplet and lepton multiplets
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Corollary 8.2 (Multiplet relabeling). Under sigma, the color-triplet
multiplet assignment transforms as

    u_R^c <-> d_R^c,                                                 (34)

and the lepton-multiplet assignment transforms as

    e_R^c <-> nu_R^c.                                                (35)

The multiplet Q_L (color-triplet, weak doublet) and L_L (color-singlet,
weak doublet), both on Omega^1, are invariant.

Proof. By Theorem 7.3, sigma swaps Omega^0 and Omega^2. Under the sector
assignment (Input 3 of HCSM-07), u_R^c occupies Omega^0 and d_R^c
occupies Omega^2; hence they are swapped. Similarly for e_R^c and
nu_R^c on the B_1 block (which carry the sector values +1 on Omega^0 and
0 on Omega^2). [Box]

8.3 Invariance of physical-prediction magnitudes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Corollary 8.3 (Physical-prediction invariance). Any framework prediction
that depends only on (i) the multiset of hypercharge magnitudes and
(ii) the D4 structure of the multiplet is invariant under sigma up to
the relabeling u_R^c <-> d_R^c and e_R^c <-> nu_R^c. In particular:

(a) The magnitudes |V_ij| of the CKM matrix are invariant:

        |V_ij| -> |V_ji| = |V_ij|.                                   (36)

(b) The magnitudes |U_ij| of the PMNS matrix are invariant.

(c) The Jarlskog invariant J flips sign:

        J = c_12 s_12 c_23 s_23 c_13^2 s_13 sin(delta) -> -J.        (37)

Proof. Under u <-> d exchange, the CKM matrix transforms as
V -> V^dagger. The magnitudes satisfy |(V^dagger)_ij| = |V_ji|; by
unitarity of V, the magnitudes |V_ij| and |V_ji| are equal (this is a
standard fact about the CKM matrix in the standard PDG phase
convention). Hence |V_ij| is invariant under sigma.

The same argument applies to the PMNS matrix U under e <-> nu exchange.

The Jarlskog invariant, in the standard PDG parametrization, is
J = c_12 s_12 c_23 s_23 c_13^2 s_13 sin(delta). Under complex
conjugation of the CKM matrix (equivalent to the u <-> d exchange
combined with a phase redefinition of the quark fields), delta -> -delta,
so sin(delta) -> -sin(delta). Hence J -> -J. The magnitudes |J| are
preserved. [Box]

Remark (Status of the flavor-sector application). The flavor-sector
predictions (CKM and PMNS matrices) are the subject of HCSM-34 [HCSM-34]
and HCSM-36 [HCSM-36]. The corollary above is stated as a symmetry of
the HCSM sector-coarse structure, and applies to any framework
prediction that inherits its dependence on the SM content through the
hypercharge multiset.

Corollary 8.4 (Labeling convention). The sign of O is a labeling
convention, fixed by the requirement Y(u_R^c) < Y(d_R^c). Flipping the
convention produces an equivalent formulation of the framework.

Proof. By Theorems 7.1 and 7.2, flipping the sign of O is equivalent to
acting with sigma. By Corollary 8.3, all magnitudes of physical
predictions are invariant under sigma. Hence the two formulations are
physically equivalent. [Box]


================================================================================
9. ANOMALY PRESERVATION
================================================================================

Corollary 9.1 (Anomaly preservation). The four one-loop anomaly
cancellation conditions

    Tr(Y) = 0,
    Tr(Y^3) = 0,
    SU(2)^2 U(1) = 0,
    SU(3)^2 U(1) = 0                                                 (38)

and the three cubic gauge anomaly cancellations are invariant under
sigma.

Proof. Under sigma, the sector hypercharge values are permuted: Omega^0
and Omega^2 exchange their values, and Omega^1 is fixed. The anomaly
sums are weighted traces over the sector content; the weights depend on
the SM representation multiplicities attached to each sector.

For the mixed SU(3)^2 U(1) anomaly, the sector-coarse form is (HCSM-08
[HCSM-08], Proposition 10.1)

    Y_0 + 2 Y_1 + Y_2 = 0.                                           (39)

Under sigma, this becomes

    Y_2 + 2 Y_1 + Y_0 = 0.                                           (40)

Since the sum is symmetric in Y_0 and Y_2, the condition is preserved.

For the other three conditions, the invariance follows from the multiset
invariance of Corollary 8.1: any symmetric function of the sector
hypercharge values that vanishes before sigma also vanishes after.
Specifically, Tr(Y), Tr(Y^3), and SU(2)^2 U(1) are all symmetric sums of
hypercharge values weighted by sector content, and the flip permutes
Omega^0 and Omega^2 without changing the multiset of values.

The cubic gauge anomalies vanish by content structure (HCSM-08, Theorem
7.1) regardless of the sector assignment, so they are trivially
invariant. [Box]

Remark (Physical reading). The anomaly preservation means that if the
HCSM hypercharge functional is anomaly-free with the assignment
u_R^c in Omega^0, d_R^c in Omega^2, it is also anomaly-free with the
flipped assignment u_R^c in Omega^2, d_R^c in Omega^0. The anomaly
cancellation does not distinguish the two assignments.


